[agent:result] stdout: The Clusters API allows you to create, start, edit, list, terminate, and
[agent:result]   delete clusters.
[agent:result] 
[agent:result]   Databricks maps cluster node instance types to compute units known as DBUs.
[agent:result]   See the instance type pricing page for a list of the supported instance types
[agent:result]   and their corresponding DBUs.
[agent:result] 
[agent:result]   A Databricks cluster is a set of computation resources and configurations on
[agent:result]   which you run data engineering, data science, and data analytics workloads,
[agent:result]   such as production ETL pipelines, streaming analytics, ad-hoc analytics, and
[agent:result]   machine learning.
[agent:result] 
[agent:result]   You run these workloads as a set of commands in a notebook or as an automated
[agent:result]   job. Databricks makes a distinction between all-purpose clusters and job
[agent:result]   clusters. You use all-purpose clusters to analyze data collaboratively using
[agent:result]   interactive notebooks. You use job clusters to run fast and robust automated
[agent:result]   jobs.
[agent:result] 
[agent:result]   You can create an all-purpose cluster using the UI, CLI, or REST API. You can
[agent:result]   manually terminate and restart an all-purpose cluster. Multiple users can
[agent:result]   share such clusters to do collaborative interactive analysis.
[agent:result] 
[agent:result]   IMPORTANT: Databricks retains cluster configuration information for terminated
[agent:result]   clusters for 30 days. To keep an all-purpose cluster configuration even after
[agent:result]   it has been terminated for more than 30 days, an administrator can pin a
[agent:result]   cluster to the cluster list.
[agent:result] 
[agent:result] Usage:
[agent:result]   databricks clusters [flags]
[agent:result]   databricks clusters [command]
[agent:result] 
